## Replacement heuristics

### First: U+FFFD does NOT always mean a Swedish letter

This is the most common mistake. U+FFFD replaces **any** character that could not
be decoded from the original encoding — not only å, ä, ö. In these files it has
been observed to represent:

| What FFFD was | How to recognise it | Replacement |
|---|---|---|
| Swedish diacritic (å/ä/ö/Å/Ä/Ö) | Sits inside a word between regular letters | The letter that makes a real Swedish word |
| Accented loanword letter (é) | End of French-borrowed word: `entr?`, `resum?`, `id?er` | `é` (`[char]0xE9`) |
| Opening quotation mark `"` | FFFD immediately before a capitalized word or start of speech | `"` |
| Closing quotation mark `"` | FFFD immediately after the last word of a title or speech | `"` |
| En dash `–` | FFFD between two numbers or two place-name halves (`1 ? 5`, `syd?Barva`) | `–` (`[char]0x2013`) or `-` |
| Hyphen `-` | FFFD joining two parts of a compound proper noun | `-` |
| Literal `?` (question mark) | At the very end of a sentence that is a genuine question | `?` (U+003F) |

**Decision order for each U+FFFD token:**
1. Is it between regular letters inside a word with no surrounding punctuation? → Swedish letter
2. Is it at the start of a quoted phrase or before a capitalized word? → opening `"`
3. Is it at the end of a word inside a title-like phrase? → closing `"`
4. Is it between two numbers or between two compound-name parts? → `–` or `-`
5. Is the sentence a genuine question and the `?` is at the very end? → literal `?`
6. Is the word a French-borrowed word ending in a vowel? → `é`
7. None of the above → use `-WithContext` before deciding; do not guess

---

### The `?r` problem — most common ambiguous case

`?r` appears hundreds of times per file and has four valid resolutions:

| Context | Replacement | Example |
|---|---|---|
| After a number | `år` | `nr 31 ?r 2008` → `nr 31 år 2008` |
| As a copula verb (subject + `?r` + predicate) | `är` | `sommaren ?r h?r` → `sommaren är här` |
| Sentence-start in ALL CAPS heading | `ÅR` | `100 ?R` → `100 ÅR` |
| Sentence-start in normal prose | `Är` | rare |

**Pitfall:** a general `${r}r` → `år` rule will silently mangle ALL CAPS headings
like `KVARNEN I BARVA 100 ÅR`. Add explicit keys for uppercase forms before the
general lowercase rule:
```powershell
$text = $text.Replace("100 ${r}R",   "100 ${A}R")   # heading: 100 ÅR
$text = $text.Replace("${r}r",       "${a}r")        # general: år  ← or är depending on file
```
Decide which is more common in the file via `-WithContext` before choosing the
default direction.

